---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import SpecCard from "../../components/article/SpecCard.astro";
import NumberedPoints from "../../components/article/NumberedPoints.astro";
import PartnerGrid from "../../components/PartnerGrid.astro";
import EmailSignupBand from "../../components/EmailSignupBand.astro";

/*
 * Written for a rec director, a school, or a business owner, not a parent.
 *
 * The whole job of this page is to make hosting feel small. A rec director
 * assumes a skateboarding program needs a skatepark, a fence, a waiver pile,
 * and a liability conversation with their solicitor. It needs a basketball
 * court. Everything here comes from docs/kb/operations.md.
 *
 * Insurance wording is the approved public phrasing. Never name the carrier,
 * the agency, the policy number, or the insured address. This repo is public.
 */

const whatWeBring = [
  { label: "Boards", body: "Twelve to fifteen skateboards, sized for the kids who sign up." },
  { label: "Coaches", body: "A USSEA-certified lead coach, plus assistants as needed to hold one coach for every six kids." },
  { label: "Safety", body: "A first aid kit, and mobile barriers to slow down a loose board. They're pool noodles on a cord, held down by weights. Simple and they work." },
  { label: "Insurance", body: "Commercial general liability written for youth recreation, including sexual abuse liability, plus accident coverage for every registered kid." },
  { label: "Signups", body: "Registration through Sawyer, unless you would rather run your own." },
  { label: "The program", body: "A real curriculum, and the same coach every single week." },
];

const promotion = [
  {
    lead: "We send you the materials.",
    body: "Print-ready flyers, the program description and details, and a pack of social graphics. Nothing for you to write or design.",
  },
  {
    lead: "You share it with your people.",
    body: "An email to your list and a post on your social channels do most of the work. Flyers up in the building help too.",
  },
  {
    lead: "We post it on our side.",
    body: "If you engage with our posts, more local families see them. That's the whole ask.",
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Partner With Ramp Up"
  description="Host a mobile youth skateboarding program at your township, school, rec center, or business. All you need is a flat space about the size of half a basketball court."
>
  <section>
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Get Involved &rsaquo; Partner With Ramp Up</p>
      <h1>All we need is a flat spot</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Ramp Up is mobile. We load the boards and the ramps into the van and
        bring the whole program to your community. Your town doesn't need a
        skatepark. It needs a basketball court.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-you-provide">What you provide</h2>
      <p>
        This is a shorter list than most people expect, so here is the whole
        thing.
      </p>

      <SpecCard
        title="The space"
        rows={[
          { label: "Ideal", body: "A basketball court. The surface and the size are both right." },
          { label: "Smallest", body: "Half a basketball court, roughly 2,000 square feet. Not much less than that." },
          { label: "Also works", body: "Tennis courts, quiet parking lots, and indoor gyms." },
          { label: "Must have", body: "A reasonably smooth surface, no live traffic, and nothing breakable right up against it." },
        ]}
      />

      <p>
        A fence isn't required. Restrooms are a nice thing to have and also
        not required. If you're picturing a fenced concrete skatepark, picture
        the gym floor instead.
      </p>

      <p>
        The one other thing we ask for is a hand getting the word out, which is
        further down this page and takes about five minutes.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-we-bring">What we bring</h2>
      <p>Everything else.</p>

      <SpecCard title="In the van" rows={whatWeBring} />

      <p>
        Every coach 18 and older carries all three Pennsylvania clearances, and
        we keep them on file. Assistant coaches under 18 are always working
        alongside a lead coach. Because our lead coaches hold First Aid and CPR
        through their USSEA certification, every session has at least one adult
        trained to handle an injury.
      </p>
      <p>
        We carry commercial general liability insurance for youth recreation
        programs, including sexual abuse liability. We're happy to provide a
        certificate of insurance. Just ask.
      </p>

      <h2 id="filling-the-program">Filling the program</h2>
      <p>
        Marketing is shared. You know your community and we know how to explain
        skateboarding to a nervous parent, so we split it.
      </p>

      <NumberedPoints points={promotion} />

      <h2 id="numbers">How many kids</h2>
      <p>
        A multi-week program runs with 6 kids and caps at 12. Free clinics go up
        to 15. If only eight families sign up, that's a good program and we're
        glad to run it.
      </p>
      <p>
        If we come up short of six, we postpone, and we tell everyone at least
        three days before the first class so nobody drives out for nothing.
        Families move to the new dates automatically, and anyone who can't make
        them gets a full refund. We also build a spare week into the schedule so
        a rained-out class can be made up instead of lost.
      </p>

      <h2 id="lead-time">When to start planning</h2>
      <p>
        About one month ahead for a free clinic, and two to three months ahead
        for a paid program. Planning in January for an April start is
        comfortable.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our favorite way to do it is both at once. Promote a free clinic for a
        month, run it, then start a six-week paid program about two weeks later.
        The clinic introduces us to your families and the paid program is where
        they actually learn to ride.
      </p>

      <h2 id="shared-spaces">If the space is shared</h2>
      <p>
        When we use part of a skatepark or another public space, we keep as much
        of it open to everyone else as we can. We're guests in your town and we
        would rather not take over the facility for an hour.
      </p>

      <h2 id="start">Start a conversation</h2>
      <p>
        Tell us about your space and roughly when you're thinking. We'll tell
        you straight away whether it works.
      </p>
      <p>
        <a class="button button--primary" href="/contact">Get in touch &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <PartnerGrid />

  <EmailSignupBand
    heading="Not ready to talk yet?"
    body="Get on the list and see what we're running around the county."
  />
</BaseLayout>
